import{_ as n,C as i,o as t,c as d,a2 as a,b as r,w as o,a as l,E as h,a3 as c}from"./chunks/framework.Ci_RTJJ5.js";const v=JSON.parse('{"title":"03 — Overview","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"chapters/03-tools-that-touch-disk/overview.md","filePath":"chapters/03-tools-that-touch-disk/overview.md"}'),p={name:"chapters/03-tools-that-touch-disk/overview.md"};function u(m,e,b,g,w,k){const s=i("Mermaid");return t(),d("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=a('<h1 id="_03-—-overview" tabindex="-1">03 — Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#_03-—-overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;03 — Overview&quot;">​</a></h1><p><em>How tools that touch disk work, and what can go wrong. Read this before you write code.</em></p><hr><h2 id="a-tool-is-a-capability-and-capabilities-have-edges" tabindex="-1">A tool is a capability, and capabilities have edges <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-tool-is-a-capability-and-capabilities-have-edges" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A tool is a capability, and capabilities have edges&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>read</code> takes a path and returns text. <code>write</code> takes a path and text. <code>bash</code> takes a command line. Each looks like a function call. Each has three edges you have to decide about before you write it:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Edge</th><th>Question</th><th>Wrong answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arguments</td><td>whose input is this?</td><td>treat it as trusted</td></tr><tr><td>Output</td><td>how much can come back?</td><td>return all of it</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>how long can it take?</td><td>wait forever</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The model writes the arguments, so they are attacker-controlled input from your program&#39;s point of view. The machine writes the output, so it can be larger than the context window. The command decides the duration, so it can outlive the conversation.</p>',7)),(t(),r(c,null,{default:o(()=>[h(s,{id:"mermaid-66",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20LR%0A%20%20%20%20M%5B%22model%22%5D%20--%3E%7C%22arguments%3A%20untrusted%20text%22%7C%20T%5B%22tool%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20T%20--%3E%7C%22bounded%20text%22%7C%20M%0A%20%20%20%20T%20%3C--%3E%7C%22files%2C%20processes%22%7C%20W%5B%22workspace%22%5D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20A%5B%22argument%20edge%3A%3Cbr%2F%3Eresolve%2C%20refuse%20escapes%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20B%5B%22output%20edge%3A%3Cbr%2F%3Ecap%20and%20mark%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20C%5B%22time%20edge%3A%3Cbr%2F%3Ebound%20and%20report%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20A%20-.-%3E%20T%0A%20%20%20%20B%20-.-%3E%20T%0A%20%20%20%20C%20-.-%3E%20T%0A"})]),fallback:o(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[l(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[2]||(e[2]=a(`<h2 id="the-workspace-boundary" tabindex="-1">The workspace boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-workspace-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The workspace boundary&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every path is resolved against a workspace root and refused if it escapes. Two rules make it hold:</p><ol><li>Resolve first, then check. <code>a/../../etc/passwd</code> contains <code>..</code>, but so do legitimate names, and a symlink defeats any string check.</li><li>Refuse absolute paths outright. The model has no reason to name one, and allowing it makes the check conditional on the machine.</li></ol><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>  requested:  ../../etc/passwd</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  joined:     /workspace/../../etc/passwd</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  resolved:   /etc/passwd</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  relative:   ../../etc/passwd          ← starts with .., refused</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The same helper serves <code>read</code> and <code>write</code>. A write escape is worse than a read escape: it changes the machine.</p><h2 id="exit-codes-are-results" tabindex="-1">Exit codes are results <a class="header-anchor" href="#exit-codes-are-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Exit codes are results&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A shell command that exits 3 has done something normal. The model asked for an operation, the operation reported failure, and the model can now decide what to do about it.</p><p>So <code>bash</code> returns text:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>exit code: 3</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>before</span></span></code></pre></div><p>and the program keeps going. Only two things are errors: the process could not start, and the user cancelled. Both mean the conversation cannot continue meaningfully.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Result</th><th>Go error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>exit 0</td><td><code>exit code: 0</code> plus output</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>exit 3</td><td><code>exit code: 3</code> plus output</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>output too long</td><td>capped plus marker</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>tool timeout</td><td><code>command timed out after 10s</code></td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>user pressed Ctrl-C</td><td>—</td><td><code>context.Canceled</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>sh</code> not found</td><td>—</td><td>start error</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="two-bounds-and-they-are-not-the-same" tabindex="-1">Two bounds, and they are not the same <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-bounds-and-they-are-not-the-same" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two bounds, and they are not the same&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>  -timeout        bounds one provider request      (chapter 01)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -tool-timeout   bounds one tool execution        (this chapter)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>They are separate because they fail differently. A slow model should not consume the budget for running a test suite, and a long test suite should not look like a slow model. Both arrive through a context, so both are cancellable, but one is the caller&#39;s decision and the other is the tool&#39;s.</p><h2 id="the-truncation-rule" tabindex="-1">The truncation rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-truncation-rule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The truncation rule&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An unbounded output does not just cost tokens. It costs the model&#39;s understanding of position: with 200 KB of log in the middle of the conversation, the important instruction from the first message is proportionally invisible.</p><p>So cap, and say what you capped:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>... [truncated: 20000 of 184320 bytes shown]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A silent cap is worse than no cap, because the model will reason about the part it thinks it saw.</p><h2 id="a-known-limitation-stated-plainly" tabindex="-1">A known limitation, stated plainly <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-known-limitation-stated-plainly" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A known limitation, stated plainly&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>exec.CommandContext</code> kills the process it started. It does not reliably kill that process&#39;s children. A command like <code>sh -c &quot;sleep 100 &amp;&quot;</code> can leave a background process behind.</p><p>Fixing it properly means process groups, which is platform-specific: on Unix, <code>Setpgid</code> plus a negative-pid kill; on Windows, a job object. That is a chapter of its own, so this course states the limitation and moves on. Chapter 08 adds cancellation for the request; the process-group gap stays documented in &quot;where next&quot;.</p><h2 id="where-the-real-implementations-go-further" tabindex="-1">Where the real implementations go further <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-real-implementations-go-further" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the real implementations go further&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>pi</th><th>crush</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Permission policy before a tool runs</td><td><code>beforeToolCall</code> hook</td><td><code>internal/permission/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Process groups and sandboxing</td><td>platform-specific</td><td><code>internal/shell/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Output handling</td><td>truncation with handles</td><td><code>internal/fsext/</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The three edges are the same everywhere. What they add is policy, and policy gets a seam in chapter 08.</p><h2 id="self-check" tabindex="-1">Self-check <a class="header-anchor" href="#self-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Self-check&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Why is resolving before checking safer than scanning the string for <code>..</code>? <em>(The workspace boundary)</em></li><li>A command exits 3. What does the model receive, and what does the program return? <em>(Exit codes are results)</em></li><li>Why are the request timeout and the tool timeout separate? <em>(Two bounds, and they are not the same)</em></li><li>Why is a silent truncation worse than a visible one? <em>(The truncation rule)</em></li><li>Name the case that would break if you killed only the direct child process, and explain why this chapter does not grade it. <em>(A known limitation, stated plainly)</em></li></ol>`,27))])}const y=n(p,[["render",u]]);export{v as __pageData,y as default};
